#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119570 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "01f32313-178c-5626-91af-110907202416" :question/math-academy-id "e-4936" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(9,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 9,-2,3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the normal vector of the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-4,7 \\rangle\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ &= ⟨9,-2, 3⟩ \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ &= 56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ &= 56 \\\\\n3x - 4y + 7z &= 56 \\\\\n3x - 4y + 7z - 56 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/id #uuid "d92eb242-104f-55cd-82dc-7620e2f4e636" :question/math-academy-id "e-4937" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,4,8)$ and is perpendicular to the line\n$\\dfrac{x+3}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}{3}=\\dfrac{z+2}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFrom the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle$. So, the normal vector of the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ &= ⟨ - 1, 4, 8⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ &= 33 \\\\\n- 5x + 3y + 2z &= 33 \\\\\n- 5x + 3y + 2z - 33 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119572 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "aa7c3400-9bc0-5f3a-84e5-f3cc2bb87f5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4938" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi$, find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0$." :question/worked-solution "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y + z + 13 &= 0 \\\\\nz &= -2x + y - 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-13$. So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi$. Note that the position vector of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\n(r - ⟨0, 0,-13⟩) \\cdot ⟨2,-1, 1⟩ &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119573 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "8a9e1070-0ca0-5f24-ac89-999c9271a91b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4939" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= v_{1} \\cdot v_{2} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2 \\\\\n&= 1 \\mid 3 \\mid - 2 \\\\\n&=-16i - 4j - 14k \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ &= ⟨ - 3, 2,-6⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ &= 124\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ &= 124 \\\\\n- 16x - 4y - 14z &= 124 \\\\\n- 16x - 4y - 14z - 124 &= 0 \\\\\n8x + 2y + 7z + 62 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5f61e67-d536-5694-b66a-9aa84d0ee15c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 5y + 5z - 16 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117797} {:answer/id #uuid "07240827-a981-5f95-9c8e-38cdca43d4a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 5y + 5z + 16 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117798} {:answer/id #uuid "29052769-126a-5866-ba29-4d94c9d0440a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x - 2y + z = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117799} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1aefd3-ac99-5205-83bf-751c14e9569a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x - 2y + z + 16 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117800} {:answer/id #uuid "69349a20-593e-5360-82ee-a5b6257317e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 5y + 5z = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5f61e67-d536-5694-b66a-9aa84d0ee15c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 5y + 5z - 16 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117797} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e6a3b15-d638-5466-a39d-2a8c03dfaae7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117796}] :question/id #uuid "93f0f0ea-5609-5473-8b26-f85225442381" :question/math-academy-id "q-33912" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1, 0, 3)$ and is parallel to the vectors $v_{1} = ⟨5, 2, 1⟩$ and $v_{2} = ⟨0, 3, 3⟩$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9283ff37-3e4f-5163-ad99-71efe40ad5f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r - ⟨0, 0, 1⟩) \\cdot ⟨ - 1,-1, 16⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117803} {:answer/id #uuid "ac70ffe8-eae8-556a-97d5-052b005eed8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r - ⟨0, 0, 1⟩) \\cdot ⟨1,-1, 4⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117804} {:answer/id #uuid "bd828451-41f3-5243-bda4-b6fab3137715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r - ⟨0, 0,-4⟩) \\cdot ⟨1,-1, 4⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117805} {:answer/id #uuid "02b09862-2f48-5ea4-bf9b-bda41a4c4028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r - ⟨0, 0, 4⟩) \\cdot ⟨1,-1, 4⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117806} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5f9c12-c888-5da9-9418-2ad33e18b29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r - ⟨0, 0,-1⟩) \\cdot ⟨1, 0, 4⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd828451-41f3-5243-bda4-b6fab3137715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r - ⟨0, 0,-4⟩) \\cdot ⟨1,-1, 4⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117805} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cadd5d9-6b27-5ebe-b532-ee330506bfa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117802}] :question/id #uuid "2d949e3c-e35b-5754-bfa5-04230aa782a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-33923" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $x - y + 4z + 16 = 0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r - p) \\cdot n = 0$."}]}
